fr:mainmatter.html:p:
The center of mass of a distribution of mass in space is the unique point at any given time where the weighted relative position of the distributed mass sums to zero.
System of particles
\vec {R} = \frac {\sum m_i \vec {r}_i}{\sum m_i}
Continuous volume
If the mass is distributed continuously, the center of mass is given by the following formula:
\vec {R} = \frac {\int \vec {r} dm}{\int dm}

Work is the energy transferred to or from an object via the application of force along a displacement.
Positive Work: The force has a component in the direction of the displacement.
Negative Work: The force has a component opposite to the direction of the displacement. Both force and displacement are vectors. The work done is given by the dot product of the two vectors, where the result is a scalar. The work can be given by line integrals:
W = \int _C \vec {F} \cdot d\vec {r}
where d\vec {r} is the tiny displacement vector along the path C. For motion along a straight line, we have a degenerated case of the line integral:
W = \vec {F} \cdot \vec {r}

The time derivative of the work is called power:
P = \frac {dW}{dt}
dW can be written as the dot product of the force and the velocity of the object dW = \vec {F} \cdot d\vec {r}, hence the power can be written as:
P = \vec {F} \cdot \frac {d\vec {r}}{dt} = \vec {F} \cdot \vec {v}
The work then can be expressed as the integral of the power over time:
W = \int _{\Delta t} P dt = \int _{\Delta t} \vec {F} \cdot \vec {v} dt

The kinetic energy of a point object, or a non-rotating rigid body depends on the 
mass of the body as well as its speed.

    E_k = \frac {1}{2} m v^2 = \frac {p^2}{2m}

From Newton's second law, the rate of change of the momentum of a particle is equal to the 
instantaneous force F acting on it

    \vec {F} = \frac {d\vec {p}}{dt}

Hence the change in momentum in time interval (t_1, t_2) can be written as:

    I = \Delta \vec {p} = \int _{t_1}^{t_2} \vec {F} dt = \int _{\vec {p_1}}^{\vec {p_2}} d\vec {p}

We read this as the impulse I is equal to the change in momentum.

A force applied perpendicularly to a lever multiplied by its distance from the lever's fulcrum (the length of the lever arm) is its torque.
\vec {\tau } = \vec {r} \times \vec {F}
The magnitude of torque is \tau = rF\sin \theta (r is the magnitude of the position vector \vec {r} and \theta is the angle between the force vector and the lever arm vector).

In three-dimensional Euclidean Space, the torque can be written as the components:
\vec {\tau } = \begin {pmatrix} \tau _x \\ \tau _y \\ \tau _z \end {pmatrix} = \begin {pmatrix} yF_z - zF_y \\ zF_x - xF_z \\ xF_y - yF_x \end {pmatrix}
The component of torque in the direction of an axis is called the torque to that axis.

The net torque determines the rate of change of the body's angular momentum.

    \vec {\tau } = \frac {d\vec {L}}{dt}

The angular momentum of a particle is defined as:

    \vec {L} = \vec {r} \times  \vec {p}

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:
Now we prove the equivalence of definitions.
\frac {d\vec {L}}{dt} = \vec {r}\times \frac {d\vec {p}}{dt} + \frac {d\vec {r}}{dt}\times \vec {p}
The rate of change of linear momentum is the force and the rate of change of position is velocity.
\frac {d\vec {L}}{dt} = \vec {r}\times \vec {F} + \vec {v}\times \vec {p}
The cross product of \vec {p} with \vec {v} is 0 because velocity and momentum are parallel. Hence we have:
\frac {d\vec {L}}{dt} = \vec {r}\times \vec {F} = \tau
